{
  "area": "dominican-republic",
  "method": "Whole country, with the dive centers' coverage running east to west and around to the north: Sosúa/Cabarete, Samaná/Las Galeras, Punta Cana/Bávaro, Bayahibe with Parque Nacional del Este (Cotubanamá) and Catalina and Saona islands, then La Caleta/Boca Chica and Juan Dolio. No official marine-park, mooring or Ministerio de Medio Ambiente dive-site list was found (searched in Spanish and English; Fundemar and the park pages publish none), so the Ministry of Tourism's own site pages (godominicanrepublic.com, one list) stood in for the official list at Sosúa, La Caleta and Boca Chica. Operator lists read: Scuba Dive Sosua, Dive Cabarete, Sosua Diving Center, Superior Dive Sosua, Las Galeras Divers (through the Wayback Machine, 2025 snapshot), Ozeanic Caribbean (Samaná town), Dressel Divers, Grand Bay of the Sea, ScubaCaribe, Pro Dive International, Coral Point Diving, Go Dive Bayahibe, ScubaFun, Dominican Divers, Ultra Marine Divers, Caribbean Divers Boca Chica and Aquatrack (Santo Domingo, which lists sites across the country). A site is counted when named on at least two of these independent lists (each operator once, the Ministry of Tourism once); everything else keeps its row with the reason. Operator lists that were not readable (Reef Oasis, SeaPro, Treasure Divers, Ocean Diving, Dolin, Scubaquatic) were not counted. Caves (Padre Nuestro, El Chicho, Cueva Taína, Cueva de Chopa) are excluded as beyond the cavern zone, Don Quico as technical depth, and the Silver Bank and Monte Cristi whale and archaeological trips as snorkel-only or permit-only. Saint George (about 40 m top-to-bottom, mostly dived at 25 to 35 m) and Piedra Bonita (a pinnacle dived at 7 to 25 m) are counted as recreational because every operator sells them to Advanced Open Water divers. Pro Dive's list pools Bavaro, Bayahibe and Saona sites without a heading, so its Saona items were assigned by the names. No list gave coordinates, so no positions are recorded. Counted 2026-10-14.",
